  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 248 PM CDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nThe upper level disturbance moving out of the southwest trough\ncontinued to lift northeast as it exits this region this\nafternoon. A cold front across our western fringes can be seen\nin observations moving into Dawson county, with one final push\nof light showers possible across the area as it tracks eastward\nthrough the evening hours. Ahead of this front, cannot\ncompletely rule out an isolated thunderstorm, but given the \nlack of instability and thunderstorm development during the \ndaytime hours thus far, the probability of this remains very \nlow. As this system exits the region late tonight, expect light\nwinds in its wake with the potential for some light fog \ndevelopment across the local area to start the day Tuesday. \nGiven the light westerly component of the wind, do not think fog\ndevelopment across the local area will be widespread or dense, \nwith the better probabilities existing across western Nebraska.\n\nFor Tuesday, expect significant cloud cover to remain in place \nacross the region, but for a dry day with a return to more mild \ntemperatures. As temperatures rise, some of the mesoscale \nmodels indicate some light shower potential late Tuesday\nafternoon/early evening, with spotty coverage and minimal QPF.\nGiven this, maintained a low end chance for showers over this\nperiod, although most of the area should remain dry.\n\nMore widespread sunshine will likely return to the local area on\nWednesday, after another chance for some morning fog across the\nlocal area. Given the more favorable light southeasterly flow \nacross the area and high SREF probs Wednesday morning, could see\nmore widespread fog to start the day. That said, after any \nmorning fog clears, Wednesday afternoon looks like a \nspectacular fall afternoon with ample sunshine, light winds, and\nhigh temperatures in the low to mid 70s across the board. \n\nTwo additional distinct chances for precipitation return through\nthe remainder of the forecast period with the first coming\nThursday night through Friday night with a disturbance moving\nout of the southwest, and a second chance late in the\nweekend/early next week with a disturbance moving out of the\nPacific Northwest. This leaves Halloween inbetween systems, and\nwhile there is a small chance for precipitation Halloween \nnight - due in part to the blending of different computer \nmodels, most (if not all) of the local area should be dry with \nmild temperatures in the 60s expected during the evening hours.",
